wiretap-server (0.1.0) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * First packaged release. The Python it replaces had no packaging at all: it
    was deployed by copying a script and a unit into a home directory.
  * On a host running that Python deployment, its unit at
    /etc/systemd/system/wiretap-server.service - the same name, and /etc
    outranks /usr/lib - is stopped and moved aside to .pre-deb. Left in place it
    would shadow this package's unit entirely: systemctl would keep acting on
    the Python and report it healthy while the packaged binary never ran. The
    new daemon is deliberately NOT started in that case; the settings have to be
    carried across by hand first.
  * The daemon runs as the system user `wiretap`, not as `pi`. An AF_CAN socket
    needs no capability, so only port 23 does - CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, and
    nothing else.
  * The disk cache moves from ~/.wiretap-server-cache.db to
    /var/lib/wiretap-server/cache.db, with its write-ahead log and without its
    wal-index, which SQLite rebuilds. The old path cannot work under this unit,
    which sets ProtectHome=true: the daemon can no longer see a home directory
    to adopt a cache from, so an upgrade during a gateway outage would otherwise
    strand captured frames that never reached a sink.
  * The unit is enabled but started only if it was already running. A fresh
    install cannot usefully run until an interface and a gateway are configured,
    and anything else is a crash loop in the journal.

 -- Alex Ferrara <alex@wiredsquare.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +1000
